More Fun Extending the C Preprocessor: Mutable State::title
<i>[David Priver], October 18th, 2026</i>

::raw
  <article>
::links
  previous post = /Fun-Extending-the-C-Preprocessor.html
  my C Compiler/Interpreter = https://github.com/drpriver/DrC


The C Preprocessor is a powerful but limited tool and is the first stage of
many in the transformation of C source code into execution or an executable.
Its mutable state has traditionally been limited to specific
<tt>#directives</tt>. <tt>__COUNTER__</tt> (now standardized in C2y) cracks
that door open by mutating state during expansion. What if we pushed that door
open the rest of the way? As I alluded to in my [previous post], you can
achieve some powerful results with only a few simple primitives.


<tt>__COUNTER__</tt>::md
  <tt>__COUNTER__</tt> is a widely implemented C Preprocessor extension, which has
  finally been standardized in the upcoming C2y standard. It expands to 0 on
  its first use, and 1 more than its previous expansion on subsequent uses.

  The usual usage of this is to generate unique suffixes within a translation
  unit, which works better than <tt>__LINE__</tt> for this purpose.
  ::pre.C
    #define CAT(A, B) CAT_(A, B)
    #define CAT_(A, B) A##B
    #define UNIQUE(prefix) CAT(prefix, __COUNTER__)
    // __LINE__ wouldn't give us a unique identifier.
    int   UNIQUE(foo); char  UNIQUE(foo);
    float UNIQUE(foo); short UNIQUE(foo);
  Expands to:
  ::pre.C
    int  foo0;  char  foo1;
    float foo2; short foo3;
  Now <tt>__COUNTER__</tt> is a very interesting builtin macro not for the
  above usage, but because it introduces something to the C-preprocessor you
  didn't have before: mutable state in macro expansions. You had mutable state
  before (what are <tt>#define</tt>, <tt>#undef</tt>, etc. if not imperatively
  mutating the state of the macro table?), but not in the middle of a macro
  expansion. What if we pulled at this thread a little bit more? What could we
  achieve if we had the power to implement <tt>__COUNTER__</tt> ourselves?

  But first a motivating example.

A Motivating Example: X Macros::md
  A powerful pattern in C is the X-macro pattern. Frequently you'll have
  some collection of symbols, values or other things that need to be kept in
  sync with each other, but you can't syntactically put them in an array of C
  structs. So as with any other programming problem you add a layer of
  indirection: create a table in the preprocessor instead and macro expand it
  in different ways based on the need.

  There is more than one way to implement the X-macro pattern, but
  a common way is the following:
  ::pre.C
    #define COMMANDS \
      X(exit, "exit the program") \
      X(help, "print the help") \
      X(reverse, "reverse the string") \
      X(exec, "exec the cmdline")
    #define X(name, help) \
      const char *name##_help = help;
    COMMANDS
    #undef X

  Used for example like this:
  cmdline.c::details
    ::pre.C
      #include <stdio.h>
      #include <string.h>
      #include <stdlib.h>
      #ifndef _WIN32
      #include <unistd.h>
      #endif
      #define STR(x) STR_(x)
      #define STR_(x) #x
      #define CAT(A, B) CAT_(A, B)
      #define CAT_(A, B) A##B
      // The X-macro.
      #define COMMANDS \
        X(exit, "exit the program") \
        X(help, "print the help") \
        X(reverse, "reverse the string") \
        X(exec, "exec the cmdline")

      #define X(name, _) int CAT(cmd_, name)(char*);
      COMMANDS
      #undef X
      enum Cmd {
        #define X(name, _) CAT(CMD_, name),
        COMMANDS
        #undef X
        CMD_COUNT,
      };
      const struct {
        const char *name; const char *help;
      } helps[] = {
        #define X(name, help) {STR(name), help},
        COMMANDS
        #undef X
      };

      int dispatch(char *cmdline){
        char *cmd;
        char *space = strchr(cmdline, ' ');
        if(space){
          *space = 0;
          cmd = cmdline;
          cmdline = space+1;
        }
        else {
          cmd = cmdline;
          cmdline = cmd+strlen(cmdline);
        }
        #define X(name, _) \
          if(strcmp(cmd, STR(name))==0) \
            return CAT(cmd_, name)(cmdline);
        COMMANDS
        #undef X
        printf("Unrecognized cmd '%s'\n", cmd);
        cmd_help("");
        return 0;
      }

      int main(){
        char buff[512];
        char *p;
        for(printf("> "), fflush(stdout);
            (p = fgets(buff, sizeof buff, stdin));
            printf("> "), fflush(stdout)
        ){
          char *nl = strrchr(p, '\n');
          if(nl) *nl = 0;
          if(!*p) continue;
          int ret = dispatch(p);
          if(ret) return ret;
        }
      }
      int cmd_exit(char *cmdline){
          printf("goodbye!\n");
          exit(0);
      }
      int cmd_help(char *cmdline){
          for(int i = 0; i < CMD_COUNT; i++)
              printf("%s: %s\n", helps[i].name, helps[i].help);
          return 0;
      }
      int cmd_reverse(char *cmdline){
          printf("reverse(%s) = ", cmdline);
          for(size_t i = strlen(cmdline); i--; )
              putchar(cmdline[i]);
          putchar('\n');
          return 0;
      }
      int cmd_exec(char* cmdline){
        #ifdef _WIN32
          printf("Not supported on windows!\n");
          return 1;
        #else
          return execl("/bin/sh", "sh", "-c", cmdline, NULL);
        #endif
      }
  This is a powerful technique, as you can see even in this toy CLI.
  In practice though, you run into several annoying problems:
  1. Giant macros with backslash-continuations are ugly. The more pieces
     of parallel data you need to support, the worse it gets.
  2. The entire X-macro has to be defined up-front. You have to pre-declare
     everything in it instead of it being discovered from your program.
  3. Conditionally including things in the X-macro list is a huge pain.
     For example, the above program should probably exclude unsupported
     commands like <tt>exec</tt> based on platform, but you'd end up having
     to have an X macro list that is the combination of two X macro
     lists and it quickly gets out of hand.

  If only we could write something like:
  ::pre.C
    #define CMD(name, help) ... magic ...
    CMD(help, "list the help")
    CMD(exit, "exit the program")
    ...
    #ifndef _WIN32
    CMD(exec, "exec the command line")
    #endif
  And get the benefits of that X-macro without its baggage?

<tt>__get()</tt> /  <tt>__set()</tt>::md
  So back to what we observed with <tt>__COUNTER__</tt>. Here we had a macro
  that mutates preprocessor state based on its expansion. What primitives would
  we need to implement something like this ourselves in the CPP?

  If it was a normal programming language, it would be pretty trivial.
  ::pre.C
    int MY_COUNTER = 0;
    int __MY_COUNTER__(void){
      int result = MY_COUNTER;
      MY_COUNTER = MY_COUNTER + 1;
      return result;
    }
  But we're in the CPP. We need:
  - The ability to retrieve data
  - The ability to compute a value.
  - The ability to store that computation back.

  Something like:
  ::pre.C
    // initialize MY_COUNTER
    __set(MY_COUNTER, 0)
    // define the "function"
    #define __MY_COUNTER__ \
      /* output this token into the token stream */ \
      __get(MY_COUNTER) \
      /* compute and store the updated value */ \
      __set(MY_COUNTER, __calc(__get(MY_COUNTER)+1))

    int x = __MY_COUNTER__; // int x = 0;
    int y = __MY_COUNTER__; // int y = 1;
  [<tt>__calc()</tt> | calc] is needed to convert the token sequence using
  preprocessor-arithmetic into a single pp-number, which you need to support
  the unique-id use case of <tt>__COUNTER__</tt>.
  ::links
    calc = https://github.com/drpriver/DrC/blob/master/EXTENSIONS.md#__calcpp-expression

  This by itself is already pretty useful. Instead of a single global
  <tt>__COUNTER__</tt> stream in your preprocessor, you can have independent
  streams, each one counting something independently. And now that you can
  implement it yourself, you can customize it. Maybe it shouldn't output any
  tokens at all, just increment a counter every time a macro is expanded which
  you retrieve in one final place. The point is you now have the power in your
  own hands instead of just a special case that the CPP provides.

  With just the primitives of <tt>__get()</tt> and <tt>__set()</tt>, you can
  already replace the need to define the X-macro all at once:

  ::pre.C
    __set(COMMANDS, ) // initialize to empty token sequence
    #define CMD(name, help) \
      __set(COMMANDS, __get(COMMANDS) X(name, help) )
    CMD(help, "list the help")
    CMD(exit, "exit the program")
    #ifndef _WIN32
    CMD(exec, "exec the command line")
    #endif
    #define X(name, help) \
      const char *name##_help = help;
    __get(COMMANDS)
    #undef X
  Which on <tt>_WIN32</tt> expands to:
  ::pre.C
    // whitespace added for legibility
    const char *help_help = "list the help";
    const char *exit_help = "exit the program";

  Which is exactly what we wanted! With two simple primitives we can register
  X-macros from the source of your program instead of defining them up front,
  implement <tt>__COUNTER__</tt> ourselves, and really, the possibilities are
  endless.

  Functional Version::details
    The above uses the common version of the X-macro, but if you like a more
    functional style you can do it like this:
    ::pre.C
      __set(COMMANDS, )
      // use preprocessor "tuples" (tokens wrapped in parens)
      // __append() simplifies the common need to add more tokens
      #define CMD(name, help) __append(COMMANDS, (name, help), )
      CMD(help, "list the help")
      CMD(exit, "exit the program")
      #ifndef _WIN32
      CMD(exec, "exec the command line")
      #endif
      // unpack the preprocessor tuple
      #define DECL(args) DECL_ args
      #define DECL_(name, help) \
        const char *name##_help = help;
        // DrC __map() extension, skips the trailing comma
        __map(DECL, __get(COMMANDS))
      #undef DECL
      #undef DECL_
    This way you don't have to name the macro ahead of time.

Implementing It::md
  ::links
    my C Preprocessor = https://github.com/drpriver/DrC
    extensions = https://github.com/drpriver/DrC/blob/master/EXTENSIONS.md#__setname---__getname--__appendname-
  In [my C Preprocessor], I have added these [extensions] as well as
  <tt>__append()</tt>.

  The grammar is something like:
  ::pre
    __get( identifier )
    __set( identifier, pp-tokens[opt] )
    __append( identifier, pp-tokens[opt] )

  The arguments do get macro-expanded, like normal arguments to function-like
  macros.

  <tt>__append()</tt> simplifies the common use case of
  adding more pp-tokens to a preprocessor variable:
  ::pre.C
    // Instead of:
    __set(key, __get(key) more tokens)
    // do:
    __append(key, more tokens)

  <tt>__set()</tt> and <tt>__append()</tt> do not expand to any pp-tokens.

  The implementation itself was fairly trivial: the preprocessor has an
  identifier-keyed dictionary with token lists as the values. <tt>__set()</tt>
  overwrites the token list, <tt>__get()</tt> retrieves the token list and
  <tt>__append()</tt> extends the token list without having to materialize the
  token list first. This feature could be extended to allow a pp-string as a key,
  which would avoid problems with macro expanding the key.

  You can use it as part of using the <tt>drc</tt> interpreter, or you can use
  the preprocessor stand-alone as <tt>drcpp</tt>.

Conclusion::md
  With the addition of two simple primitives of <tt>__get()</tt> and <tt>__set()</tt>,
  we can greatly increase the power and utility of the C Preprocessor and
  enable techniques that were simply too cumbersome to do before. The
  possibilities are numerous:
  - Independent counter streams
  - Distributed function/symbol registration
  - Distributed struct field registration:
    - Different headers can declare they need some shared <tt>ctx</tt> struct
      to include a certain field. The header actually defining the struct
      can then retrieve those fields directly into the struct's body.
  - Deferred code:
    - Accumulate code that must be called at program startup into
      a preprocessor variable and insert it as the first line of
      <tt>main()</tt>.
  - and many more.

  However, can we get even more powerful? That's best
  left for the future.
::raw
